plied curve and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)&=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y(t)=\\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\P(x(t),y(t))&=8 \\cos^{3}{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\x\\prime(t)&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y\\prime(t)=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=\\left(8 \\cos^{3}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+8 \\cos^{4}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReduce the fourth power by applying the cosine double-angle identity twice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos^4t&=\\left(\\frac{1+\\cos(2t)}2\\right)^2\\\\&=\\frac14\\left(1+2\\cos(2t)+\\frac{1+\\cos(4t)}2\\right)\\\\&=\\frac{3+4\\cos(2t)+\\cos(4t)}8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the other powers, use $\\sin^2t=\\frac{1-\\cos(2t)}2$.\n\nFor terms containing a remaining factor $\\cos t$, use $u=\\sin t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nIntegrate these terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 8 \\cos^{4}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=3 t + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{4}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=t - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[3 t + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{4}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=6 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=6 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=0\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[t - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=2 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=2 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the contributions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=(6 \\pi) + (0) + (2 \\pi)=8 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722933 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09bc08ba-06e8-5099-8583-780f791fafba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722912} {:answer/id #uuid "3674cee3-d64e-5dcf-b1e7-0f705c86ffaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722919} {:answer/id #uuid "76bc55fd-c364-560c-a055-07172045b6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722925} {:answer/id #uuid "7900604d-b94a-5f36-97f7-1b6dad26f362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722927} {:answer/id #uuid "8d915320-bb5d-55f8-96a6-273f68b6466b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d915320-bb5d-55f8-96a6-273f68b6466b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722929} :answer-field/id #uuid "1742538d-6dee-528b-b008-ec12fdf447e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b22ec42e-fd2b-5152-ad20-53f4787949d8" :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- x)\\,\\mathbf i+(- x + y^{3})\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf i+2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y\\prime-Q x\\prime)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)&=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y(t)=2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\P(x(t),y(t))&=- \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=8 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\x\\prime(t)&=- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y\\prime(t)=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=\\left(- \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(8 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=8 \\sin^{4}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}-\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}-2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReduce the fourth power by applying the cosine double-angle identity twice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^4t&=\\left(\\frac{1-\\cos(2t)}2\\right)^2\\\\&=\\frac14\\left(1-2\\cos(2t)+\\frac{1+\\cos(4t)}2\\right)\\\\&=\\frac{3-4\\cos(2t)+\\cos(4t)}8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the other powers, use $\\cos^2t=\\frac{1+\\cos(2t)}2$.\n\nFor terms containing a remaining factor $\\cos t$, use $u=\\sin t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nIntegrate these terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int - 2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- t - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- \\frac{\\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}}{2}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 8 \\sin^{4}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=3 t - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{4}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- t - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=- 2 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=- 2 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- \\frac{\\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}}{2}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=0\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[3 t - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{4}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=6 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=6 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the contributions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=(- 2 \\pi) + (0) + (6 \\pi)=4 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1141d621-0382-5e67-9744-026ee011b4c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 10 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722913} {:answer/id #uuid "1b22baa8-30b2-5537-954a-eb5c2fa411de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722916} {:answer/id #uuid "39ffb854-4460-5629-ac1a-bc9fd0b3599e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722920} {:answer/id #uuid "72cd86c9-cb95-5c12-b213-905748431faf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 2 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722924} {:answer/id #uuid "af0e346a-5e44-512a-bde6-d7b0fa02efec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 4 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72cd86c9-cb95-5c12-b213-905748431faf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 2 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722924} :answer-field/id #uuid "063b69fb-fde4-5e97-ab8a-6f7b67a36f6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d3d0fed7-cdac-594e-aaba-d98abf4b714d" :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- x^{3} + 2 y)\\,\\mathbf i+(2 y)\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf i+\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y\\prime-Q x\\prime)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)&=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y(t)=\\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\P(x(t),y(t))&=2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 8 \\cos^{3}{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\x\\prime(t)&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y\\prime(t)=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 8 \\cos^{3}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}-8 \\cos^{4}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReduce the fourth power by applying the cosine double-angle identity twice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos^4t&=\\left(\\frac{1+\\cos(2t)}2\\right)^2\\\\&=\\frac14\\left(1+2\\cos(2t)+\\frac{1+\\cos(4t)}2\\right)\\\\&=\\frac{3+4\\cos(2t)+\\cos(4t)}8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the other powers, use $\\sin^2t=\\frac{1-\\cos(2t)}2$.\n\nFor terms containing a remaining factor $\\cos t$, use $u=\\sin t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nIntegrate these terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int - 8 \\cos^{4}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- 3 t - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{4}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=\\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=2 t - \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- 3 t - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{4}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=- 6 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=- 6 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[\\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=0\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[2 t - \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=4 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=4 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the contributions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=(- 6 \\pi) + (0) + (4 \\pi)=- 2 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e5edbe8-ab46-5e5b-acff-b74403c445c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 2 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722944} {:answer/id #uuid "25798190-e73b-5714-a5a6-8e9e5b80ea06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 4 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722946} {:answer/id #uuid "2b7afb6b-66ba-5a31-abe3-a43788bc7661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722948} {:answer/id #uuid "5d50202a-9f97-5417-9261-4581c5ef66d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722953} {:answer/id #uuid "bb18377d-1fe4-5867-8308-0e95601b2cd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25798190-e73b-5714-a5a6-8e9e5b80ea06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 4 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722946} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b08a523-8a43-5c93-9229-4d48d35229ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0df30e86-f073-5593-bdc6-3b53917a8b86" :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- 2 x + e^{y})\\,\\mathbf i+(e^{x})\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf i+2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y\\prime-Q x\\prime)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)&=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y(t)=2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\P(x(t),y(t))&=e^{2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=e^{\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}},\\\\x\\prime(t)&=- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y\\prime(t)=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=\\left(e^{2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(e^{\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=2 e^{2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+e^{\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}-4 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the other powers, use $\\cos^2t=\\frac{1+\\cos(2t)}2$.\n\nFor the exponential term in $\\sin t$, use $u=2\\sin t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=2\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nFor the exponential term in $\\cos t$, use $v=1\\cos t$, $\\mathrm{d}v=-1\\sin t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nIntegrate these terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int - 4 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- 2 t - \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int e^{\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- e^{\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 2 e^{2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=e^{2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- 2 t - \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=- 4 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=- 4 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- e^{\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=- e\\\\&\\quad-\\left(- e\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[e^{2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=1\\\\&\\quad-\\left(1\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the contributions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=(- 4 \\pi) + (0) + (0)=- 4 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d1b8315-511f-5136-b38f-2c7f6530586c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722943} {:answer/id #uuid "2a46eaaa-1b69-5ef0-8add-bae60f94e57b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722947} {:answer/id #uuid "81723d00-6f25-5e91-ac47-d86320c0ea9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 12 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722957} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a6cdfc-960b-5ad4-9e01-429c8c50dc2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722959} {:answer/id #uuid "abe06c50-7e26-5c84-9997-9284d1e8b52b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 8 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d1b8315-511f-5136-b38f-2c7f6530586c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722943} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3ec6177-ef3f-5e7b-9d59-d9826c024b86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "103091fd-dd2b-566c-9213-2061712bc48e" :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- 2 x y + x)\\,\\mathbf i+(- 2 x^{2} - y)\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf i+3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y\\prime-Q x\\prime)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)&=4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y(t)=3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\P(x(t),y(t))&=- 24 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 32 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\x\\prime(t)&=- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y\\prime(t)=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=\\left(- 24 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 32 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=- 12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}-200 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+12 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the other powers, use $\\cos^2t=\\frac{1+\\cos(2t)}2$, $\\sin^2t=\\frac{1-\\cos(2t)}2$.\n\nFor terms containing a remaining factor $\\sin t$, use $u=\\cos t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=-\\sin t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nIntegrate these terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int - 200 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=\\frac{200 \\cos^{3}{\\left(t \\right)}}{3}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 12 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=6 t + 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int - 12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- 6 t + 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[\\frac{200 \\cos^{3}{\\left(t \\right)}}{3}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=\\frac{200}{3}\\\\&\\quad-\\left(\\frac{200}{3}\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[6 t + 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=12 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=12 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- 6 t + 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=- 12 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=- 12 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the contributions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=(0) + (12 \\pi) + (- 12 \\pi)=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13be49ae-9c2b-59dd-a7f7-774b861330f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722941} {:answer/id #uuid "42d10cba-114d-50db-8927-fff4b960bcf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 6 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722951} {:answer/id #uuid "484d390b-471a-56fa-8647-bc11b5ddbf48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722952} {:answer/id #uuid "a51026e2-e9d3-5c4a-9326-8e3cd92b107e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722958} {:answer/id #uuid "fa41caf0-9e83-5b2a-9814-32d22c7a1ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13be49ae-9c2b-59dd-a7f7-774b861330f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722941} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd3d8ef8-37af-5995-bdaa-df07eb9a00af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6780666f-c0a7-5762-a62d-72ac10e57996" :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x^{2} + x)\\,\\mathbf i+(2 y + 1)\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf i+2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y\\prime-Q x\\prime)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)&=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y(t)=2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\P(x(t),y(t))&=9 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 1,\\\\x\\prime(t)&=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y\\prime(t)=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=\\left(9 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 1\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+18 \\cos^{3}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the other powers, use $\\cos^2t=\\frac{1+\\cos(2t)}2$, $\\sin^2t=\\frac{1-\\cos(2t)}2$, $\\cos^3t=(1-\\sin^2t)\\cos t$.\n\nFor terms containing a remaining factor $\\cos t$, use $u=\\sin t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nIntegrate these terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 18 \\cos^{3}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- 6 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} + 18 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=3 t + \\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=6 t - 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- 6 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} + 18 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=0\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[3 t + \\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=6 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=6 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[6 t - 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=12 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=12 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=-3\\\\&\\quad-\\left(-3\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the contributions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=(0) + (6 \\pi) + (12 \\pi) + (0)=18 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722955 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ea7cd2-d98b-5a73-bf08-fa17cef247ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722942} {:answer/id #uuid "20d1c9b7-0a92-5fff-89ae-24e0a275213a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722945} {:answer/id #uuid "31516523-5bbc-5bb7-ad33-8f0db7fe4929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722949} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ae152b-e881-5516-8201-18060557bf10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 12 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722963} {:answer/id #uuid "d614416e-d05f-523f-8e44-1ebdf5eddfb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 6 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3ae152b-e881-5516-8201-18060557bf10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 12 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722963} :answer-field/id #uuid "732fc06a-94a5-57ba-ad33-7d4f3e645209" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b3918b9-3b1d-5a45-ad30-bc9af119ca76" :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- x - y^{3})\\,\\mathbf i+(x + 2)\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf i+4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y\\prime-Q x\\prime)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)&=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y(t)=4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\P(x(t),y(t))&=- 64 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2,\\\\x\\prime(t)&=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y\\prime(t)=4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=\\left(- 64 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=- 256 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+9 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}-12 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the other powers, use $\\cos^2t=\\frac{1+\\cos(2t)}2$.\n\nFor terms containing a remaining factor $\\cos t$, use $u=\\sin t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nIntegrate these terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int - 12 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- 6 t - 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int - 256 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- 64 \\sin^{4}{\\left(t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 9 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=\\frac{9 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}}{2}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- 6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- 6 t - 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=- 12 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=- 12 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- 64 \\sin^{4}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=0\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[\\frac{9 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}}{2}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=0\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- 6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=-6\\\\&\\quad-\\left(-6\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the contributions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=(- 12 \\pi) + (0) + (0) + (0)=- 12 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723237 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e1d0969-7569-5abc-8d6f-f70c19bfa306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723235} {:answer/id #uuid "f64f7050-8ade-5153-973d-255f478e5001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f64f7050-8ade-5153-973d-255f478e5001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723259} :answer-field/id #uuid "6820da9c-9b6c-54f2-bcd2-0e5baf2bbdc2" :answer-field/key "$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\langle-\\frac{y}{4}+\\cos(x^2),\\;\\frac{3x}{4}+y^3\\rangle$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723243} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "619aa1c2-c1e0-5b14-9c54-1a825d19b1a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723241} {:answer/id #uuid "93a09025-dae7-5cdd-a05e-b1baf34a86f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93a09025-dae7-5cdd-a05e-b1baf34a86f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723249} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef0d38a5-b910-5577-8c05-8f72d15bf05f" :answer-field/key "$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\langle y\\sin x,\\;x+\\cos x\\rangle$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723257} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28d480cc-6f9a-580c-a35e-5bcd8728563a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723236} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0f491a-15c7-5e5f-a190-25c7ed67281c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca0f491a-15c7-5e5f-a190-25c7ed67281c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723252} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd8e0e7b-dadf-52d4-b2d7-38a40c162df3" :answer-field/key "$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\langle e^y-y,\\;xe^y+y^2\\rangle$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e23c414-24c1-5857-b26b-73978a4e623d" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve in $\\mathbb{R}^2$, and let $D$ be the region it encloses. Which of the following vector fields make\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\operatorname{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nfor every such curve?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nAll the displayed functions have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb{R}^2$. To give area for every such region, the scalar curl must be identically $1$. We differentiate with respect to the indicated variable, holding the other variable constant.\n\nFor the first field,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=e^y-1,\\\\\nQ_x&=e^y,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=e^y-(e^y-1)=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo select the first field. The term $y^2$ in $Q$ is constant with respect to $x$.\n\nFor the second field,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=\\sin x,\\\\\nQ_x&=1-\\sin x,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=1-2\\sin x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not identically $1$, so do not select this field. Its curl equals $1$ when $x=0$, but equality at a few points does not meet the requirement for every region.\n\nFor the third field,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=-\\frac14,\\\\\nQ_x&=\\frac34,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\frac34-\\left(-\\frac14\\right)=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe terms $\\cos(x^2)$ in $P$ and $y^3$ in $Q$ disappear in these partial derivatives. Select the third field.\n\nTherefore, select the **first and third fields**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "188bacbe-61f5-5d4a-9b81-da6acb0a4781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723234} {:answer/id #uuid "3b117928-e255-53f2-ad2d-b562cea5e5c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723238} {:answer/id #uuid "63cfd52c-9faa-5c26-9858-bba8cac20170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723242} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ee5c35-d74b-5043-82a4-7afd0689ab22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723253} {:answer/id #uuid "deb54d82-e8a1-525f-b152-48f69b00c5e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "188bacbe-61f5-5d4a-9b81-da6acb0a4781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723234} :answer-field/id #uuid "541658f9-2ce0-54c6-a6f1-a3da41a9bf8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73455c85-8d8a-59f2-b1b2-5583e594bbf2" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve in $\\mathbb{R}^2$, and let $D$ be the region it encloses. Which of the following pairs of functions make\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\operatorname{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nfor every such curve?\n\nI. $P(x,y)=y+x^2$ and $Q(x,y)=x+y^2$\n\nII. $P(x,y)=-y+x^2$ and $Q(x,y)=y^2$\n\nIII. $P(x,y)=2y$ and $Q(x,y)=3x+y^2$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nAll the displayed functions have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb{R}^2$. To give area for every such region, the scalar curl must be identically $1$. We differentiate with respect to the indicated variable, holding the other variable constant.\n\n- **I:** $P_y=1$ and $Q_x=1$, so $Q_x-P_y=1-1=0$. This pair does not give area.\n- **II:** $P_y=-1$ and $Q_x=0$, so $Q_x-P_y=0-(-1)=1$. This pair gives area.\n- **III:** $P_y=2$ and $Q_x=3$, so $Q_x-P_y=3-2=1$. This pair also gives area.\n\nThe $x^2$ terms in $P$ and the $y^2$ terms in $Q$ disappear in the required partial derivatives. Therefore, the answer is **II and III only**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91559043-236a-53c5-ab41-bbc1c4fd0708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723248} {:answer/id #uuid "dbaf9a60-78b0-521a-9563-9bac1f92e3ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbaf9a60-78b0-521a-9563-9bac1f92e3ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723254} :answer-field/id #uuid "71620b83-c270-56dc-a992-18ffbe6360d5" :answer-field/key "$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\langle y\\sin(xy)-y,\\;x\\sin(xy)\\rangle$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723244} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "764ff935-2fd1-574c-a7c5-3f3edded6dc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723246} {:answer/id #uuid "c37bb0eb-eaf8-586a-9361-1ec5516a9405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "764ff935-2fd1-574c-a7c5-3f3edded6dc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723246} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1fd3d14-9b0e-5d52-82b1-4a02df79b66d" :answer-field/key "$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\langle y\\sin(xy)-y,\\;x\\sin(xy)+x^2\\rangle$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723250} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54adae31-d756-520d-8fa7-8cfccf7f4a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723240} {:answer/id #uuid "de4c4b12-afc3-5de8-b46f-dca9f4961854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de4c4b12-afc3-5de8-b46f-dca9f4961854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723255} :answer-field/id #uuid "f221ac14-ad8c-54c3-bf4b-f7fe8a6eff11" :answer-field/key "$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\langle ye^{xy}-\\frac{y}{2},\\;xe^{xy}+\\frac{x}{2}\\rangle$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "82988560-78da-543c-bcbd-6d66d55cd082" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve in $\\mathbb{R}^2$, and let $D$ be the region it encloses. Which of the following vector fields make\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\operatorname{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nfor every such curve?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nAll the displayed functions have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb{R}^2$. To give area for every such region, the scalar curl must be identically $1$. We differentiate with respect to the indicated variable, holding the other variable constant.\n\nFor the first field, use both the product and chain rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=\\sin(xy)+xy\\cos(xy)-1,\\\\\nQ_x&=\\sin(xy)+xy\\cos(xy),\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe two variable terms cancel, leaving $1$. Select this field.\n\nFor the second field,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=e^{xy}+xy e^{xy}-\\frac12,\\\\\nQ_x&=e^{xy}+xy e^{xy}+\\frac12,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\frac12-\\left(-\\frac12\\right)=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, both variable terms cancel. Select this field.\n\nFor the third field,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=\\sin(xy)+xy\\cos(xy)-1,\\\\\nQ_x&=\\sin(xy)+xy\\cos(xy)+2x,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=1+2x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe trigonometric terms cancel, but $2x$ remains. This curl is not identically $1$; for example, it is $3$ at $x=1$. Do not select this field. Agreement on the line $x=0$ is insufficient for every region in $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\nTherefore, select the **first and second fields**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723270 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "Correct: $-y(t)x^{\\prime}(t)=\\sin t\\,e^{\\cos t+\\sin t}$." :answer/id #uuid "0201b7a3-c215-56a4-857c-1cab955c9432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin t\\,e^{\\cos t+\\sin t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723261} {:answer/feedback "This is $x(t)y^{\\prime}(t)$; the supplied field requires $-y(t)x^{\\prime}(t)$." :answer/id #uuid "18a75a02-2477-5e1e-a063-790c251eed46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos t\\,e^{\\cos t+\\sin t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723263} {:answer/feedback "The differential is $\\mathrm dx$, so differentiate $e^{\\cos t}$, not $e^{\\sin t}$." :answer/id #uuid "ad474ad7-0c53-525d-9123-87e655f86660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin t\\,e^{2\\sin t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723278} {:answer/feedback "The minus in $P=-y$ cancels the minus in $x^{\\prime}(t)$." :answer/id #uuid "b6611f74-fafe-50c1-929b-85ee81f6c2bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin t\\,e^{\\cos t+\\sin t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723280} {:answer/feedback "Include $y(t)=e^{\\sin t}$ as well as the derivative of $x(t)$." :answer/id #uuid "d945391c-8569-53db-88cf-adc7bfb5f490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin t\\,e^{\\cos t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/feedback "Correct: $-y(t)x^{\\prime}(t)=\\sin t\\,e^{\\cos t+\\sin t}$." :answer/id #uuid "0201b7a3-c215-56a4-857c-1cab955c9432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin t\\,e^{\\cos t+\\sin t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723261} :answer-field/id #uuid "375f83d4-3720-5e4a-bf37-68916a259df6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f0f81a5-6dbb-59b3-a728-9806382f2020" :question/problem "![Exponentially transformed circle enclosing D](images/44/4408d1fcee1d168c6ab21ea53cf24845093bad9cc608299a36f0510c1636cc03.png)\n\nLet $D$ be enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle e^{\\cos t},e^{\\sin t}\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2\\pi.\n$$\nUsing $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0$, the area can be written as\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA\n=\\int_0^{2\\pi} f(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nWhich expression is the pointwise integrand obtained from this $P$, $Q$ and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign appropriate to the orientation shown? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a counterclockwise boundary. This also follows from the parametrization: at $t=0$ it starts at $(e,1)$ and moves upward. The map $(u,v)\\mapsto(e^u,e^v)$ preserves the orientation and simplicity of the unit circle.\n\nThe chosen field has scalar curl\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\nGreen's theorem therefore gives\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\oint_C(-y)\\,\\mathrm dx.\n$$\nDifferentiate $x(t)$ by the chain rule:\n$$\nx^{\\prime}(t)=-\\sin t\\,e^{\\cos t}.\n$$\nSubstituting both the coefficient and the differential gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(t)&=-y(t)x^{\\prime}(t)\\\\\n&=-e^{\\sin t}\\left(-\\sin t\\,e^{\\cos t}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\sin t\\,e^{\\cos t+\\sin t}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence the required setup is\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin t\\,e^{\\cos t+\\sin t}\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nUsing $x\\,\\mathrm dy$ instead would give a different pointwise integrand. That belongs to the other unit-curl field $P=0$, $Q=x$, rather than the field specified here."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723273 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "Clockwise orientation requires negating $x(t)y^{\\prime}(t)$, cancelling its minus sign." :answer/id #uuid "2708ea7a-5fe2-509f-8331-827a1449dbd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\sin^6 t\\cos^4 t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723265} {:answer/feedback "The derivative of $\\cos t$ supplies another factor of $\\sin t$." :answer/id #uuid "326c1d0d-e563-5eb8-9efa-d2c5878732fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sin^5 t\\cos^4 t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723267} {:answer/feedback "This comes from $y(t)x^{\\prime}(t)$. The given $P=0$, $Q=x$ requires $-x(t)y^{\\prime}(t)$." :answer/id #uuid "404751ad-4c8c-5b29-b260-6e8cfc2ee9e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cos^6 t\\sin^4 t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723269} {:answer/feedback "The power rule supplies a factor of $5$." :answer/id #uuid "7c0b470a-7af3-587c-beb9-9f9aaacd9f0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin^6 t\\cos^4 t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723276} {:answer/feedback "Correct: $-(\\sin^5 t)(-5\\cos^4 t\\sin t)=5\\sin^6 t\\cos^4 t$." :answer/id #uuid "ad68cd7b-a868-5198-a08d-42ad6858346d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sin^6 t\\cos^4 t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/feedback "Correct: $-(\\sin^5 t)(-5\\cos^4 t\\sin t)=5\\sin^6 t\\cos^4 t$." :answer/id #uuid "ad68cd7b-a868-5198-a08d-42ad6858346d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sin^6 t\\cos^4 t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723279} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f8096f6-3b9e-5f3a-b72f-3adea6e47d70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6f54923e-3f52-56e0-b4fc-d16f17711487" :question/problem "![Clockwise power curve enclosing D](images/10/10f4094edb09afc7908bd0cd3df089387c8f5b762ea5ef86da152d1c9d08b5e4.png)\n\nLet $D$ be enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\sin^5 t,\\cos^5 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2\\pi.\n$$\nUsing $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x$, the area can be written as\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA\n=\\int_0^{2\\pi} f(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nWhich expression is the pointwise integrand obtained from this $P$, $Q$ and parametrization using Green's theorem, with its sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$? Do not evaluate the integral.\n\n*Hint: $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "The curve is simple and piecewise smooth, with cusps at its axis intercepts. It is traversed clockwise. For the given field,\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1-0=1.\n$$\nBecause the orientation is negative, area is the negative of the circulation:\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=-\\oint_C\\left(P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy\\right)=-\\oint_C x\\,\\mathrm dy.\n$$\nThe chain rule gives\n$$\ny^{\\prime}(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(\\cos^5 t)=-5\\cos^4 t\\sin t.\n$$\nSubstitute into the orientation-adjusted expression:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(t)&=-x(t)y^{\\prime}(t)\\\\\n&=-(\\sin^5 t)(-5\\cos^4 t\\sin t)\\\\\n&=5\\sin^6 t\\cos^4 t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThus\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\int_0^{2\\pi}5\\sin^6 t\\cos^4 t\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nOne minus sign comes from clockwise orientation and the other from differentiating $\\cos t$. They cancel. The powers of $\\sin t$ add to $6$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "Correct: $(3\\cos t)(2\\cos t)=6\\cos^2 t$." :answer/id #uuid "27893805-ee5e-5593-9a1e-87faaa22df7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\cos^2 t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723266} {:answer/feedback "The boundary is positively oriented, so there is no extra minus sign." :answer/id #uuid "6372839d-6c05-5674-85f5-69290abe81be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\cos^2 t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723271} {:answer/feedback "Include the factor $2$ from $y^{\\prime}(t)$." :answer/id #uuid "6c1f9637-a669-5feb-88ba-c5e7cfa90c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cos^2 t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723272} {:answer/feedback "Use the supplied $y^{\\prime}(t)=2\\cos t$, not $y(t)=2\\sin t$." :answer/id #uuid "7bba2f3f-2a91-5866-b5db-91e67ff32e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sin^2 t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723275} {:answer/feedback "Multiply $x(t)$ by $y^{\\prime}(t)$, not by $y(t)$." :answer/id #uuid "7d1e68f9-db32-5a34-9765-86a80423e133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sin t\\cos t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/feedback "Correct: $(3\\cos t)(2\\cos t)=6\\cos^2 t$." :answer/id #uuid "27893805-ee5e-5593-9a1e-87faaa22df7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\cos^2 t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723266} :answer-field/id #uuid "126753dc-6425-5506-82b2-ce2bf9ef20ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71a2d3f1-83bb-5f88-bc4b-cc5c5588c1d0" :question/problem "![Counterclockwise ellipse enclosing D](images/1d/1de9544b8da50f6280920adf2292da517a844b8b4143f58f646da0a01ffad16c.png)\n\nThe positively oriented curve $C$ encloses $D$ and is parametrized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3\\cos t,2\\sin t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2\\pi.\n$$\nUse $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x$. Their scalar curl is $1$, so Green's theorem gives\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\oint_C x\\,\\mathrm dy=\\int_0^{2\\pi} f(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nHere $x(t)=3\\cos t$ and $y^{\\prime}(t)=2\\cos t$.\n\nWhich expression is the pointwise integrand $f(t)=x(t)y^{\\prime}(t)$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For this counterclockwise boundary, Green's theorem applies with a positive sign because\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1-0=1.\n$$\nIn $\\oint_C x\\,\\mathrm dy$, replace $x$ by $x(t)$ and $\\mathrm dy$ by $y^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt$. Thus\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(t)&=(3\\cos t)(2\\cos t)\\\\\n&=6\\cos^2 t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\int_0^{2\\pi}6\\cos^2 t\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nThe requested expression is $6\\cos^2 t$. Although $6\\sin^2 t$ has the same integral over this interval, it is not the pointwise product $x(t)y^{\\prime}(t)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22ffa0e3-1fe2-537d-8430-759b9a23b021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723319} {:answer/id #uuid "5cea9d0a-bbfc-5651-8cae-50bbee4d2119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723325} {:answer/id #uuid "607613f3-b27f-5130-b86b-cf37de8bf4d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723326} {:answer/id #uuid "96e61847-8f52-5a48-9288-cd7e6d0ec886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723329} {:answer/id #uuid "99902c7e-e1b3-50f4-b4aa-6dcbcdf98aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cea9d0a-bbfc-5651-8cae-50bbee4d2119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723325} :answer-field/id #uuid "e668f30a-8c60-5b49-b203-8b3d2127cde5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c889808-d4d5-5f92-91c9-b5d551e84b2f" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is parametrized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2\\cos t,\\sin t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi.\n$$\nIt traverses the boundary of $D$ once counterclockwise. Use Green's theorem with $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0$ to find the area of $D$.\n\n![The ellipse with intercepts (plus or minus 2, 0) and (0, plus or minus 1), traversed counterclockwise.](images/29/29f6e1c802a936c7f5a2fb8d55ff228dc1504a00ea663710f43d4880d2b4cfca.png)" :question/worked-solution "The chosen field has unit scalar curl:\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\nSince $C$ is positively oriented, Green's theorem gives\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\iint_D 1\\,dA=-\\oint_C y\\,dx.\n$$\nHere $y(t)=\\sin t$ and $x'(t)=-2\\sin t$. Thus\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=-\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin t(-2\\sin t)\\,dt\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(1-\\cos 2t)\\,dt\\\\\n&=\\left[t-\\tfrac12\\sin 2t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\\n&=2\\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe area is $2\\pi$ square units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2265f4fb-d409-5126-92db-4ed232247a9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723318} {:answer/id #uuid "2787dc2f-4721-5aa1-8ec8-159cb2c9e483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723320} {:answer/id #uuid "71e2fbcf-c67e-5154-a7c4-52249593289b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723327} {:answer/id #uuid "bb550371-d4b8-56c0-8dce-b88773aa5153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac83" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723334} {:answer/id #uuid "fec102aa-bbdb-5135-88f1-c8bbbebfb972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2787dc2f-4721-5aa1-8ec8-159cb2c9e483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723320} :answer-field/id #uuid "2adb8d58-09cd-566e-b197-41aac69ee03f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b518b083-be00-5d3e-b24a-145d69570fc6" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\sin t,\\sin 2t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\leq t\\leq\\pi.\n$$\nThe arrows indicate the direction of increasing $t$.\n\n![A loop to the right of the y-axis, from the origin along its upper branch and back along its lower branch, with clockwise arrows.](images/9f/9f80d82b0d4c4c7e7d8d59ffbbd059fa57d02d254af79b514cdef81a5a81d816.png)" :question/worked-solution "The path starts at $(0,0)$, follows the upper branch to $(1,0)$, and returns along the lower branch. It is clockwise, so it has negative orientation.\n\nChoose $P=-y$ and $Q=0$. Their scalar curl is $1$. Green's theorem with the orientation correction gives\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=-\\oint_C(P\\,dx+Q\\,dy)=\\oint_C y\\,dx.\n$$\nSince $x'(t)=\\cos t$ and $y(t)=\\sin 2t$,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\int_0^\\pi\\sin 2t\\cos t\\,dt\\\\\n&=2\\int_0^\\pi\\sin t\\cos^2t\\,dt.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSet $u=\\cos t$, so $du=-\\sin t\\,dt$. The limits become $1$ and $-1$. Hence\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=-2\\int_1^{-1}u^2\\,du\n=\\frac{2}{3}\\left[u^3\\right]_{-1}^{1}\n=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\nThe area is $\\frac43$ square units. Using $-\\oint_C y\\,dx$ without correcting the clockwise orientation would give the negative of the area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "322e5dd6-124c-518a-82d9-be153acabe0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723322} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc25c73-efc4-59c8-9fa0-7e3eb4b22134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723323} {:answer/id #uuid "8675fdaa-1aa8-516b-af66-f691afb9ef7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac67" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723328} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8e4e1e-7caf-5bed-95ce-8315f43704a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723331} {:answer/id #uuid "af60c0a2-670b-5a6e-9379-226f530fd839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa8e4e1e-7caf-5bed-95ce-8315f43704a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723331} :answer-field/id #uuid "e163de5e-45b0-5ba3-8e42-d5102d4e3f20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ccf9d517-cbc9-57a8-b072-2b540d5d9bee" :question/problem "The boundary $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ encloses a region $D$, where\n\n- $C_1$ is parametrized by $\\mathbf r_1(t)=\\langle t^3,-t^4\\rangle$ for $-1\\leq t\\leq1$;\n- $C_2$ is parametrized by $\\mathbf r_2(t)=\\langle-t,-1\\rangle$ for $-1\\leq t\\leq1$.\n\nTraverse $C_1$ and then $C_2$ in the directions of increasing parameter. Use Green's theorem to find the area of $D$.\n\n![The curved upper boundary C1 runs from (-1,-1) through (0,0) to (1,-1). The horizontal lower boundary C2 returns to (-1,-1).](images/d5/d58009320fabb8ea68bbc67a0c62198965cd5297ba287f5e27e8d45d064269eb.png)" :question/worked-solution "The endpoints join: $C_1$ goes from $(-1,-1)$ to $(1,-1)$ through $(0,0)$, and $C_2$ returns from $(1,-1)$ to $(-1,-1)$. The boundary is clockwise.\n\nChoose $P=0$ and $Q=x$, whose scalar curl is $1$. Because the orientation is negative,\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=-\\oint_C x\\,dy\n=-\\int_{C_1}x\\,dy-\\int_{C_2}x\\,dy.\n$$\nOn $C_1$, $x=t^3$ and $dy=-4t^3\\,dt$. On $C_2$, $y=-1$ is constant, so $dy=0$. Therefore\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=-\\int_{-1}^1t^3(-4t^3)\\,dt-\\int_{-1}^1(-t)\\cdot0\\,dt\\\\\n&=4\\int_{-1}^1t^6\\,dt\\\\\n&=4\\left[\\frac{t^7}{7}\\right]_{-1}^1\\\\\n&=4\\left(\\frac17+\\frac17\\right)=\\frac87.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe straight segment contributes zero for this choice of field; it is still needed to close the boundary. The area is $\\frac87$ square units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c87735e-88b1-5640-b036-c22560c07f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723384} {:answer/id #uuid "206d00a0-5b96-527f-b3f0-83423cc76b7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723385} {:answer/id #uuid "70e1ed73-40e5-5974-aef0-74e2874def6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723391} {:answer/id #uuid "8bdc0970-a468-527a-9ad4-4a9350afc8bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723395} {:answer/id #uuid "b0487bc2-1e9a-50b5-8d54-18a78be270e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bdc0970-a468-527a-9ad4-4a9350afc8bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723395} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a6b0558-89e9-5f5d-a508-b8bf4e72ef0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88031f31-cab5-573e-a4e9-055f8a400828" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle x+\\sin y,\\ y+\\cos x\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le1,\\ 0\\le y\\le x^2\\}.\n$$\n\n![Region below y=x squared, above y=0, between x=0 and x=1.](images/35/3572e500529bb0e2e4a21ed94d0f31a1b5edc3406a572d7173225f4fc440a606.png)\n\nFor $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, use\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on an open set containing $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ pointing outward, Green's theorem in flux form gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds\n=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx\n=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=x+\\sin y$ and $Q=y+\\cos x$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=1,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=1.\n$$\n\nThus the divergence is $2$. The supplied bounds describe a type I region. Integrating first with respect to $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_0^1\\int_0^{x^2}2\\,dy\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1[2y]_{y=0}^{y=x^2}\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1 2x^2\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{2x^3}{3}\\right]_0^1=\\frac{2}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $\\frac{2}{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723399 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15668dda-7696-5f19-b25a-15919989f290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723383} {:answer/id #uuid "3da3c44e-b9b1-5b3b-98f3-011b99e1d18d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723390} {:answer/id #uuid "779110dc-e931-5733-a776-b84ab51861dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723393} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d21e33-12e3-5854-8884-cd45a95c89a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723396} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3f241f-2d49-5ca1-8e73-c8e5571a6b09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "779110dc-e931-5733-a776-b84ab51861dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723393} :answer-field/id #uuid "02186b64-f746-5154-b90c-f724c9ac8cc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7742b6e-e4b1-5d3b-8dba-8fb177fa9dfc" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle x^2+\\sin y,\\ xy+e^x\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by $y=x^3$, $y=0$, and $x=1$.\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on an open set containing $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ pointing outward, Green's theorem in flux form gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds\n=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx\n=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=x^2+\\sin y$ and $Q=xy+e^x$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=2x,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=x.\n$$\n\nThe divergence is $3x$. The curve $y=x^3$ meets $y=0$ at $x=0$. From there to $x=1$, the region lies above $y=0$ and below $y=x^3$:\n\n![Region below y=x cubed, above y=0, and left of x=1.](images/26/26cbf8624da7845c8c2022254893e53b6dc85561f9878dc5989d11eaad035bac.png)\n\nHence its type I description is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le1,\\ 0\\le y\\le x^3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_0^1\\int_0^{x^3}3x\\,dy\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1[3xy]_{y=0}^{y=x^3}\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1 3x^4\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{3x^5}{5}\\right]_0^1=\\frac{3}{5}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $\\frac{3}{5}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723400 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3579dde4-a72b-5fb9-bca4-cd1fed78a97f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723386} {:answer/id #uuid "3c6a4ad6-02b7-53da-b5ae-f4a2f5211ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723389} {:answer/id #uuid "74feabf5-5d53-5fac-ba42-80fe170f3248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723392} {:answer/id #uuid "ada4befa-1f27-5fb4-b3c1-946cd3d9d343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723397} {:answer/id #uuid "e32895d4-568c-5d70-9b4c-ed4e3168b425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e32895d4-568c-5d70-9b4c-ed4e3168b425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723401} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bc57a6b-f404-5072-b792-62b4bf4370d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d0292e79-5824-5607-a46b-9571a2ddc059" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle x^2+\\sin y,\\ xy+e^x\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by $x=-2y$, $x=\\sqrt{y}$, and $y=1$.\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on an open set containing $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ pointing outward, Green's theorem in flux form gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds\n=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx\n=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=x^2+\\sin y$ and $Q=xy+e^x$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=2x,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=x.\n$$\n\nThe divergence is $3x$. Since $\\sqrt{y}\\ge0$ and $-2y\\le0$ for $y\\ge0$, the two curves meet only at $(0,0)$. The top boundary is $y=1$. A horizontal slice runs from $x=-2y$ to $x=\\sqrt{y}$:\n\n![Region between x=-2y on the left and x=square root of y on the right, from y=0 to y=1; a horizontal slice is shown.](images/6e/6e28b8a2920142f7847d2813fc350248b8bbdf4c1fced6d89ba823ce778e483a.png)\n\nThus a type II description avoids splitting the region:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le y\\le1,\\ -2y\\le x\\le\\sqrt{y}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_0^1\\int_{-2y}^{\\sqrt{y}}3x\\,dx\\,dy\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1\\left[\\frac{3x^2}{2}\\right]_{x=-2y}^{x=\\sqrt{y}}\\,dy\\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{2}\\int_0^1(y-4y^2)\\,dy\\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{2}\\left[\\frac{y^2}{2}-\\frac{4y^3}{3}\\right]_0^1\\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\frac12-\\frac43\\right)=-\\frac54.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $-\\frac54$. Its negative sign means the net flux points into the region. The left side's negative contribution outweighs the right side's positive contribution; the region is not symmetric about the $y$-axis."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3328ec98-6ed5-5ff2-af82-630d194a33cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{1} 4xy-2y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723445} {:answer/id #uuid "77227bba-4e73-5242-bd37-cfbf27a323e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{1} 4y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723449} {:answer/id #uuid "ab312025-1ac0-5924-9f01-f952f250a4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{1} y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723450} {:answer/id #uuid "bcea1e47-cdca-5842-a318-d8eb9ceca0a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{-1}^{2} 4y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723453} {:answer/id #uuid "c1fff077-e829-5372-a876-0ffc76f13e84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{1} -x^2-2xy-y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77227bba-4e73-5242-bd37-cfbf27a323e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{1} 4y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723449} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2a79a71-2f6d-5478-93ee-7ca8ae2314f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0b18778a-08d6-5e00-9f87-b7ecf0571527" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0)$, $(2,0)$, $(2,1)$, and $(-1,1)$, traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle x^2y+xy^2,\\,-xy^2+y^3\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, the flux form of Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C\\left(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\right)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$, and the counterclockwise boundary has the required positive orientation.\n\nThe region $D$ and its boundary $C$ are shown below.\n\n![Rectangle with horizontal range -1 to 2 and vertical range 0 to 1.](images/a4/a418f22532577cce1962726ecf6ed61537b25c2df0c71de395ccdef462d68e55.png)\n\nThe component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=2xy+y^2,\\qquad\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=-2xy+3y^2.\n$$\n\nAdding, rather than subtracting, cancels the mixed terms:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=4y^2.\n$$\n\nThe rectangle is $D=\\{(x,y):-1\\leq x\\leq2,\\ 0\\leq y\\leq1\\}$. Integrating with respect to $y$ first gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\int_0^1 4y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe inner bounds belong to $y$ and the outer bounds belong to $x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ffd8019-e621-58a7-8bcc-a35473aac4b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{x^2}^{2-x^2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723443} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab1adf6-8689-5928-a7e9-88c809011c26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{0}^{2} 3y\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723448} {:answer/id #uuid "b38673aa-2bf1-5f87-b151-08166638fa35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{x^2}^{2-x^2} 4xy-y\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723452} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f5fd5f-77c4-51a7-860f-9a7add479f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{2-x^2}^{x^2} 3y\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723457} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ac109b-d087-5eb1-8632-973b6e0628f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{x^2}^{2-x^2} 3y\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5ac109b-d087-5eb1-8632-973b6e0628f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{x^2}^{2-x^2} 3y\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723459} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf881aff-8e38-5b8f-9165-e26550ef6442" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "285e9d64-72cd-5e39-85f2-e9e1f4bcd7f0" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the boundary of the finite region enclosed by the parabolas $y=x^2$ and $y=2-x^2$, traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle x^2y+xy,\\,-xy^2+y^2\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral in the order $\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, the flux form of Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C\\left(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\right)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$, and the counterclockwise boundary has the required positive orientation.\n\nFirst find where the boundaries meet:\n\n$$\nx^2=2-x^2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x^2=1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=-1\\text{ or }x=1.\n$$\n\nBoth intersection points have $y=1$. Between these $x$-values, the lower boundary is $y=x^2$ and the upper boundary is $y=2-x^2$.\n\n![Region between an upward parabola and a downward parabola, meeting at (-1,1) and (1,1).](images/1e/1ea32c48979cb2b4e5370b599d184b16ab1f37525b886c2ce3161f459c0232d6.png)\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\leq x\\leq1,\\ x^2\\leq y\\leq2-x^2\\}.\n$$\n\nFor the field components,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=2xy+y,\\qquad\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=-2xy+2y,\n$$\n\nso the divergence is $3y$. Combining this integrand with the bounds in the requested order gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{x^2}^{2-x^2}3y\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing constant bounds $0\\leq y\\leq2$ would instead include points outside the curved region."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723446 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a3a171-b9b3-5878-a11b-58c10cb59065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723439} {:answer/id #uuid "0af4ab27-6d5f-5ca1-9ae6-6093b9fddd82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y^2+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723440} {:answer/id #uuid "13c37bf9-8cf9-5069-b761-da9655076eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y-3y^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723442} {:answer/id #uuid "b11c605f-0842-5f05-b77e-cc48a21ab4c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723451} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d96b79-318d-5e32-a643-08e1003fcbdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02a3a171-b9b3-5878-a11b-58c10cb59065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723439} :answer-field/id #uuid "59c4def3-aea2-54bf-9e67-d6fb850227eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b7baccd-5c3b-5a79-9d34-124d9aab163d" :question/problem "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle x^2+y^3,\\,2xy+y^2\\rangle\n$$\n\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0)$, $(1,0)$, and $(0,2)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $\\mathbf n$ is the outward unit normal, and $D$ is the enclosed region. Calculate $f(x,y)$." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, the flux form of Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C\\left(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\right)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$, and the counterclockwise boundary has the required positive orientation.\n\nThe region is shown below.\n\n![The triangular region bounded by the coordinate axes and the line through (1,0) and (0,2).](images/c3/c3c617522488e6ebf47dd2b2a52c43a1fe06ccaef8cd1e8752ab4df5e2d4bd64.png)\n\nHere $P=x^2+y^3$ and $Q=2xy+y^2$. Differentiate each component with respect to its matching coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=2x,\\qquad\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=2x+2y.\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x+(2x+2y)=4x+2y.\n$$\n\nThe triangle specifies the integration region; its bounds are not needed to find the integrand."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12634b2c-8b2c-5560-bdca-b3dcb7e21ced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723466} {:answer/id #uuid "5426e5c9-985a-5c2b-bd43-70b6c7c5d08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5426e5c9-985a-5c2b-bd43-70b6c7c5d08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723479} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ec50a16-06c9-5e75-bf98-5b1c55d5deab" :answer-field/key "**III**" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723485} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a33d05d4-5446-5748-bafe-40f4a87b44b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723489} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b387b3-6951-509e-8db1-617848c87ff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0b387b3-6951-509e-8db1-617848c87ff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723502} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdc483d2-7579-590e-9309-7ea97815f899" :answer-field/key "**II**" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723494} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83695c80-d0d3-5f20-a6e0-635978ca6da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723486} {:answer/id #uuid "c2d7adfe-c19a-5b3c-b32d-b4d89fd8d469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83695c80-d0d3-5f20-a6e0-635978ca6da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723486} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3a57454-3de7-5762-bb42-0a4cd00f2fe0" :answer-field/key "**I**" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "036d0184-fc42-5d78-9838-afc59c045bba" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve in $\\mathbb{R}^2$, and let $D$ be the region it encloses. Which of the following vector fields make\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\operatorname{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nfor every such curve?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n**I.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{I}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}x^{2} - y + \\frac{e^{x + 2 y}}{2}\\\\y - e^{x + 2 y}\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n\n**II.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{II}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}x^{2} - 2 y \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\\\x + \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n\n**III.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{III}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}x + 2 y \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + \\frac{y}{2}\\\\\\frac{3 x}{2} + \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nAll the displayed functions have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb{R}^2$. For the circulation to equal area for every such region, the scalar curl must be identically $1$. Hold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative.\n\n**I.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=e^{x + 2 y} - 1,\\\\\nQ_x&=- e^{x + 2 y},\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(- e^{x + 2 y}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(e^{x + 2 y} - 1\\right)\\\\\n&=- 2 e^{x} e^{2 y} + 1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not identically $1$: at $(x,y)=(0,0)$ its value is $-1$. Do not select **I**.\n\n**II.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)},\\\\\nQ_x&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 1,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 1\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar curl is identically $1$, so select **II**.\n\n**III.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + \\frac{1}{2},\\\\\nQ_x&=2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + \\frac{3}{2},\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + \\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar curl is identically $1$, so select **III**.\n\nTherefore, select **II and III only**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84f19d00-e734-549b-ac3e-934faab345f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723487} {:answer/id #uuid "cecf409a-c948-52cd-85dd-1e442b42cf47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84f19d00-e734-549b-ac3e-934faab345f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723487} :answer-field/id #uuid "4799b5d1-a4ea-5a2c-81dc-bb43dc06f442" :answer-field/key "**III**" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723475} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5502eb4-8f40-5755-b263-7a6aaaee0f63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723490} {:answer/id #uuid "de9b8bcb-12a4-50a4-8c6b-a7d7df65f97d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de9b8bcb-12a4-50a4-8c6b-a7d7df65f97d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723498} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d80ba48-a9cd-50ad-847b-b27241c4cdf4" :answer-field/key "**II**" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723476} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43dd9372-9b63-5012-bac7-c5d636ec0f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723474} {:answer/id #uuid "4e636a2a-d64b-53d2-8433-c9b55325f119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43dd9372-9b63-5012-bac7-c5d636ec0f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723474} :answer-field/id #uuid "707a5378-2519-5022-bb7c-0c7d36e8729a" :answer-field/key "**I**" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c1d101b-21c9-5b00-92e6-b08ec08e728b" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve in $\\mathbb{R}^2$, and let $D$ be the region it encloses. Which of the following vector fields make\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\operatorname{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nfor every such curve?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n**I.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{I}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}x^{2} + y + e^{x + 2 y}\\\\2 x + 2 e^{x + 2 y} + \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n\n**II.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{II}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\frac{e^{x + 2 y}}{2}\\\\x + y - e^{x + 2 y}\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n\n**III.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{III}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}- 2 x y^{3} + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\\\- 3 x^{2} y^{2} + x + y\\end{pmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nAll the displayed functions have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb{R}^2$. For the circulation to equal area for every such region, the scalar curl must be identically $1$. Hold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative.\n\n**I.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=2 e^{x + 2 y} + 1,\\\\\nQ_x&=2 e^{x + 2 y} + 2,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(2 e^{x + 2 y} + 2\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(2 e^{x + 2 y} + 1\\right)\\\\\n&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar curl is identically $1$, so select **I**.\n\n**II.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=e^{x + 2 y},\\\\\nQ_x&=- e^{x + 2 y} + 1,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(- e^{x + 2 y} + 1\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(e^{x + 2 y}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 2 e^{x} e^{2 y} + 1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not identically $1$: at $(x,y)=(0,0)$ its value is $-1$. Do not select **II**.\n\n**III.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=- 6 x y^{2},\\\\\nQ_x&=- 6 x y^{2} + 1,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(- 6 x y^{2} + 1\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(- 6 x y^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar curl is identically $1$, so select **III**.\n\nTherefore, select **I and III only**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0981dedd-b96b-516f-8c66-ed4e65b6b331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723461} {:answer/id #uuid "0a350931-5ba4-5be0-96cf-1807c1187e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723463} {:answer/id #uuid "753873c3-5e89-58a9-b23f-a1211229d715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723483} {:answer/id #uuid "7afb4e16-73c3-5c57-bb81-2983d4961eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723484} {:answer/id #uuid "b212153b-ed28-5664-8678-63f6cd9f1eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0981dedd-b96b-516f-8c66-ed4e65b6b331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723461} :answer-field/id #uuid "eea3e3a7-860f-588d-9813-9f574144da89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d5d43da-0cc8-52bb-8d45-55a1347c9e00" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve in $\\mathbb{R}^2$, and let $D$ be the region it encloses. Which of the following vector fields make\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\operatorname{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nfor every such curve?\n\n**I.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{I}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}2 x y^{3} + \\frac{y}{2} + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\\\3 x^{2} y^{2} + x\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n\n**II.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{II}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}- 2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - \\frac{y}{2} + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\\\\\frac{x}{2} + y^{2} + 2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n\n**III.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{III}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}x - e^{x + 2 y}\\\\- x + y^{2} - 2 e^{x + 2 y}\\end{pmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nAll the displayed functions have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb{R}^2$. For the circulation to equal area for every such region, the scalar curl must be identically $1$. Hold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative.\n\n**I.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=6 x y^{2} + \\frac{1}{2},\\\\\nQ_x&=6 x y^{2} + 1,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(6 x y^{2} + 1\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(6 x y^{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not identically $1$: at $(x,y)=(0,0)$ its value is $\\frac{1}{2}$. Do not select **I**.\n\n**II.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - \\frac{1}{2},\\\\\nQ_x&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{1}{2},\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar curl is identically $1$, so select **II**.\n\n**III.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=- 2 e^{x + 2 y},\\\\\nQ_x&=- 2 e^{x + 2 y} - 1,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(- 2 e^{x + 2 y} - 1\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(- 2 e^{x + 2 y}\\right)\\\\\n&=-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not identically $1$: at $(x,y)=(0,0)$ its value is $-1$. Do not select **III**.\n\nTherefore, select **II only**."}]}
